{"h": {"measure": "R", "k": 20}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 107, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When edge 0 is none, node 1 is dead so edges 2 and 4 carry nothing; adding a conv revives those paths."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Replacing none on edge 0 reactivates node 1 and its downstream edges, giving large gains as in experiment 16."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "If edge 0 is none, node 1 outputs zero, so any operation on edge 4 has no input to process."}, {"edit_edge": 2, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Node 2 reaches the output only through edge 5, so a conv on edge 2 is useless when edge 5 is none."}, {"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Edge 1 feeds node 2, which is disconnected from the output when edge 5 is none."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "lower", "why": "Removing the direct residual skip from input to output hurts more than removing a pooling op on edge 3."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "lower", "why": "Replacing the residual skip with pooling destroys the identity shortcut, as experiment 6 shows."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Swapping lossy pooling for an identity shortcut on the input-to-output edge is consistently beneficial."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Replacing parameter-free pooling with a 3x3 conv on edge 4 gives large gains, as experiment 1 shows."}, {"edit_ed